---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "95/100", label: "Highest complaint intensity" },
  { value: "17", label: "Independent venue types" },
  { value: "6", label: "Failure modes, six fixes" },
  { value: "3B", label: "Monthly active users at stake" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Live dashboard", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Feed_Fatigue_Dashboard.html" },
  { label: "Deck (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Instagram_Feed_Fatigue_Audit.pdf" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Loops drive people off the platform, ahead of ads"
  description="Three years of public complaint signal about Instagram, sorted into six failure modes with a product fix for each. The loudest problem and the most damaging one turn out to be different. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Instagram Feed Fatigue Audit"
  period="April 2026"
  tint="research"
  hook="Complaints about a feed stuck on one topic carry the language of leaving. Complaints about advertising carry the language of putting up with it. Product teams optimise against the second one."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          Instagram is among the most valuable advertising surfaces ever built, carrying three billion
          monthly users, projected annual ad revenue above eighty billion dollars, and the highest
          revenue per user of any major social platform. It is also a product whose own head described
          the main feed as dead in a year-end memo at the close of 2025.
        </p>
        <p>
          A platform in that position has no shortage of complaint data and very little structure over
          it. The work here was to turn three years of public signal into something a product manager
          could act on in a planning cycle: a ranked set of distinct failure modes, each one traced to
          evidence, each one paired with a fix that fits inside the architecture that already exists.
          I led the research and synthesis on a team of four.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          Six failure modes came out of the corpus, ranked by complaint intensity. Social graph
          collapse leads at <strong>95 out of 100</strong>, sustained from 2022 through 2025 without
          resolution, followed by the Reels surface invasion at 88, the stuck interest loop at 85,
          advertising and suggested-content overload at 78, disturbing content surfacing at 62, and
          commerce sameness at 58. Each was confirmed across independent venues rather than inferred
          from a single community.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result that reorders a roadmap sits underneath that ranking. Loop complaints and
          advertising complaints read completely differently. People describing a feed locked onto one
          topic talk about deleting the app. People describing too many ads express resignation and
          keep scrolling. The louder problem and the more expensive one are different problems, and
          most product assumptions have that the wrong way around.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three further findings cut against standard reads of the platform. Research on Gen Z
          preferences shows that cohort driving video consumption on TikTok while wanting photographs
          and posts on Instagram, which means the video pivot has been replacing what they came for
          with something they already get elsewhere. Millennial favourability toward Reels rose from
          31% to 47% at the same time as qualitative complaints rose, which resolves once you separate
          Reels as entertainment from Reels displacing the friend feed. And the drift users describe is
          corroborated by the company's own earnings calls, moving from a stated thirty percent of feed
          content AI-recommended to above fifty percent by the first quarter of 2024. People reading
          their feeds have been reading the product strategy accurately.
        </p>
        <p>
          One of the six needs no algorithmic work at all. In December 2025 the right-swipe gesture to
          direct messages was replaced by a Reels shortcut, which is a product decision rather than a
          ranking outcome, and restoring it absorbs the loudest current complaint the moment it ships.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious approach to a complaint corpus is volume with sentiment scoring on top, and it
          fails here for a reason specific to this problem. Volume measures how many people are talking
          on whichever venue happens to be busiest, and sentiment scoring collapses distinct grievances
          into a single unhappy number. A product manager cannot ship against a sentiment score. What
          they need is which mechanism is breaking, for whom, and what changes it.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the corpus was read rather than counted, across seventeen distinct venue types spanning
          2022 to 2025, and sorted into mechanisms that behave differently from one another. Intensity
          rather than frequency became the ranking variable, because a mechanism can be described by
          fewer people in far more final terms. That choice is what surfaced the loop result, which a
          volume ranking would have buried underneath advertising.
        </p>
        <div class="pull">
          Every mode had to survive corroboration against primary corporate disclosure before it
          entered the set: earnings call transcripts, sworn testimony from the May 2025 FTC trial, and
          public company statements. User complaint alone establishes that people are unhappy.
          Complaint plus the company confirming the underlying change establishes what is actually
          happening.
        </div>
        <p>
          Each mode then had to produce a fix that fits inside the existing architecture, because a
          recommendation requiring a new surface is a recommendation nobody funds. The friend-feed
          collapse gets a toggle serving the first ten posts of a session from followed accounts before
          recommendations resume. The stuck loop gets an interest-decay model with a fourteen-day
          half-life, one post in fifteen drawn from an adjacent cluster, and a Not Interested signal
          that suppresses a topic for thirty days rather than hiding a single post. The gesture change
          gets reverted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h-sub">The signal dashboard</h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          Built to show what tracking this properly would look like from the inside, with the six
          modes, their evidence and their proposed fixes filterable in one view.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src="/media/Linh_Le_Feed_Fatigue_Dashboard.html"
                title="Instagram feed fatigue signal dashboard" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p class="embed-note">
        <a href="/media/Linh_Le_Feed_Fatigue_Dashboard.html">Open the dashboard in its own tab</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          This is a public signal layer. There is no behavioural data behind it, no session logs, and
          no access to the metrics that would turn any of these findings into a quantified business
          case. It identifies where to look and stops short of proving impact.
        </p>
        <p>
          The central finding is the one most exposed by that boundary. Users say the stuck loop drives
          them to abandon sessions, and confirming it requires exactly the data the analysis lacks:
          whether a Not Interested event followed by continued same-topic viewing predicts session end.
          A yes turns the finding into a funded roadmap item. A no sends the hypothesis back for
          revision.
        </p>
        <p>
          One major venue was unavailable during collection. Every pattern reported here replicated
          across the remaining sources, which is why the set stands, and a corpus assembled with that
          venue included might weight the modes differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          The window closes in late 2025, and the platform kept moving. A feature launched days before
          submission generated enough confusion that turning it off became one of the most-searched
          Instagram phrases in the country overnight, which sits outside this dataset and points at the
          same underlying pattern.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
